\\documentclass{article}
\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,hyperref,url}
\\title{Do contracts discourage permitted trades?\\\\An unfinished connexion between markets and cooperation}
\\author{}
\\date{}
\\begin{document}
\\maketitle

\\begin{abstract}
This account connects \\emph{Competitive Market Behavior of LLMs} (Q) with \\emph{Commitment To Cooperation With Self-Negotiated Contracts} (P). The peers asked whether contracts could improve language-model trading, then narrowed the question to whether contractual wording makes agents infer restrictions that the rules do not impose. They established limits on payment-based explanations, feasible cooperation in P, and the distinction between market surplus and price convergence. They also corrected an overstrong reward bound: allowing payments to a nonfinisher may enlarge the set of outcomes that benefit both players. The thread ended at PAUSE because these results did not establish the proposed shared cause, and the controlled agent trials needed to test it had not been run.
\\end{abstract}

\\section{Introduction}

Q studies language models acting as buyers and sellers in a double auction, where either side can post an offer \\cite{Q}. Each agent can trade once per round. A buyer who offers at least the current asking price trades immediately at that asking price; the seller's case is symmetric. Both parties then leave the round. Q reports inefficient allocations and agents making small price adjustments despite opportunities to trade profitably. Its question is whether a familiar market institution works well when language models replace human traders.

P studies bargaining and navigation in Colored Trails \\cite{P}. Players spend coloured chips to move towards a goal and can help each other by exchanging chips or covering moves. Regular trading exchanges chips directly. In the alternative Pay-for-Partner setting, a promise to cover a later move can be refused when payment falls due. P studies contracts that automate tile payments, natural-language agreements interpreted during play, and point transfers conditional on finishing. These arrangements can improve cooperation, but some contract conditions also suppress further trade.

The initial connexion was to bring P's contracts into Q's market. Ada and Emmy rejected a simple explanation based on making settlement credible: Q already executes accepted trades automatically. They instead pursued a question about representation. Could wording an existing obligation as a contract make an agent wrongly treat it as a restriction on other profitable actions? P supplies agreements that can leave further coordination necessary. Q supplies an already enforced market in which wording could be changed without adding protection against later refusal. This is a proposed comparison, not a demonstrated common mechanism.

\\section{What was done}

The peers first compared the rules and the reported behaviour. In P's regular-trading results on mutually dependent boards, natural-language contracts give normalised joint reward of \\(0.64\\), compared with \\(0.93\\) without contracts. Normalisation divides the players' combined score by P's benchmark of \\(140\\). Against programmatic tile contracts, natural-language contracts have similar mean executed coverage, about \\(2.6\\) moves, but fewer trades: \\(1.95\\) against \\(3.28\\). These results suggested that contract use could discourage useful activity. The peers kept them separate from the delayed Pay-for-Partner setting, where refusal to honour a promise is a distinct problem.

They next checked what contracts could change economically. Ada reduced a restricted kind of bilateral payment in Q to an effective transaction price. Emmy constructed chip exchanges that let both players finish in P, and examples showing that efficient allocation in Q need not imply similar transaction prices. Ada checked the constructions independently. These calculations helped separate failures to find a feasible gain from the absence of such a gain.

The peers then designed a comparison that would hold executable terms fixed and vary their display. For Q, it would describe an unfilled order either as an order record or as a conditional obligation. A disclosed final trading opportunity would remove the possibility that declining current profit was sensible waiting. Separate questions would test which actions the agent believed were permitted, and whether a permission clarification corrected its error.

Their source checks also narrowed the possible novelty. Hantel's author summary already describes price anchors, market failures, and placebo and debiasing controls \\cite{hantel}. Ada inspected that summary rather than the full linked paper; the associated SSRN URL remained a search lead \\cite{ssrn}. Sajja and colleagues' \\emph{Evaluating Rational Contracting in Natural Language}, or ContractSim, already studies contracts as policy constraints and evaluates rational performance \\cite{contractsim}. These checks did not establish that the narrower proposed experiment was new.

Finally, Emmy reopened the reward calculation. The earlier claim that \\(140\\) was an unconditional maximum in P was too strong. Concentrating chips in a single finisher could exceed it, and a points contract might compensate the other player for not finishing. Both peers incorporated this correction. Their attempted check of P's implementation failed, so the revised claim remained conditional on the written rules.

\\section{Results and their support}

\\subsection{A same-trade payment can be an effective price}

Let \\(v\\) be a buyer's value, \\(c\\) a seller's cost, \\(p\\) the transaction price, and \\(d\\) a net payment from that buyer to that seller. Assume that \\(d\\) is triggered only by their same atomic trade, with no financing constraint or additional obligation. With quasilinear utility, meaning that money enters payoffs linearly, their payoffs \\(u_B\\) and \\(u_S\\) are
\\[
u_B=v-p-d,\\qquad u_S=p+d-c.
\\]
An effective price \\(p_{\\mathrm{eff}}=p+d\\) therefore reproduces the allocation and realised payoffs. Combined surplus is still \\(u_B+u_S=v-c\\).

Ada gave this reduction in \\path{ada/research-note.md}; Emmy accepted its qualified form in \\path{emmy/boundaries.md}. It establishes equivalence of realised outcomes under the assumptions. It does not establish equivalence of strategies. Payments tied to particular partners can reorder offers, commitments can change information or available actions, and payments triggered by third-party trades create different incentives. Thus the reduction does not justify treating every contract as a relabelled price.

\\subsection{Ordinary chip exchange reaches the both-finish bound}

In P, each player begins with fourteen chips of their own colour and two green chips. A six-move shortest route consumes five red or blue chips and a green chip for the goal. Exchanging five red chips for five blue chips gives each player nine own-colour chips, five opposite-colour chips and two green chips. Both can then afford every shortest route. Each finishes with ten chips and scores \\(20+5(10)=70\\).

The corresponding bound follows by counting resources. Let \\(R_{\\mathrm{joint}}\\) denote the sum of the two final scores. If both players finish, at least twelve of their initial thirty-two chips are consumed. The goal awards total forty points and each remaining chip is worth five, so
\\[
R_{\\mathrm{joint}}\\leq 2(20)+5(32-12)=140.
\\]
Without point transfers, a nonfinisher receives zero. Since both no-interaction baselines are nonnegative, strictly improving on both baselines requires both players to finish. The same bound therefore applies to strict mutual improvement without point transfers.

On an asymmetric board, take Red to be the player who can finish alone and Blue the player who cannot. Exchanging five red chips for six blue chips leaves Red with nine red, six blue and two green chips, and Blue with five red, eight blue and two green chips. Both can still take any shortest route. Red scores \\(75\\) and Blue scores \\(65\\), strictly exceeding their respective no-interaction baselines of at most \\(70\\) and zero.

Emmy's \\path{emmy/verify_bounds.py} checked all \\(3432\\) balanced boards and \\(20\\) shortest paths per board against five inventories, including the concentration case below. Its \\(343200\\) checks support resource feasibility. Ada independently checked the swap bounds. These are feasible constructions, not claims that agents choose them, that bargaining leads to them, or that they are equilibria.

\\subsection{Compensating a nonfinisher changes the bound}

Under P's written arbitrary-quantity exchange rule, Red can give one red chip for all fourteen blue and two green chips held by Blue. Red then holds thirteen red, fourteen blue and four green chips; Blue holds only one red chip. Red can finish along a shortest route with twenty-five chips left and score \\(145\\). Blue fails and scores zero. This refutes an unconditional \\(140\\) bound under those rules, though it does not benefit Blue.

Now suppose a programmatic points contract pays a recipient who does not finish. Red can first promise Blue ten points upon Red's own completion. The same exchange then yields \\((135,10)\\), where the first component is Red's score and the second is Blue's. On asymmetric boards, both strictly beat their no-interaction baselines and joint reward is \\(145\\). This outcome does not improve both players' scores relative to the ordinary exchange outcome \\((75,65)\\): Blue receives less.

Emmy proposed the correction and Ada independently confirmed its arithmetic and textual basis. It means that point contracts may expand the set of strictly baseline-improving rewards by compensating noncompletion. The earlier claim that P's gains must concern behaviour alone was therefore withdrawn. Joint reward, individual improvement and the number of finishers must be reported separately.

The implementation qualification is substantial. P's prompt says that a nonfinisher receives zero total points, which needs reconciliation with the point-transfer rule. Chip quantities, scoring order and termination also need checking. Ada could not open P's anonymous repository \\cite{prepo}; its API returned \\texttt{not\\_connected} \\cite{papi}. The finite resource check does not establish how that code handles the construction, nor does it establish a repository defect.

\\subsection{Market surplus and price convergence differ}

In Q, surplus means buyer value minus seller cost, summed over completed trades. The ranked gains are \\(2.5,2,1.5,1,0.5,0\\), so maximum total surplus is \\(7.5\\). Five positive-gain trades suffice to attain it. Emmy paired the five highest-value buyers with the five lowest-cost sellers, setting prices to the sellers' costs: \\(0.75,1,1.25,1.5,1.75\\). This gives full surplus despite a price-dispersion coefficient of approximately \\(41.46\\) under Q's measure of distance from its competitive price of \\(2\\).

Conversely, a single trade between a buyer with value \\(3.25\\) and a seller with cost \\(0.75\\), at price \\(2\\), has zero price dispersion but obtains only one third of maximum surplus. Emmy checked both examples in the same script, and Ada checked maximum surplus independently. These feasible allocation and payment examples show why price convergence cannot substitute for surplus measurement. They do not dispute Q's measured efficiency losses or assert that these examples describe typical play.

\\section{Objections and unresolved explanations}

The behavioural evidence did not isolate representation. Equal mean covered moves in P does not imply equal contract quality, routes, inventories, timing or remaining gains. Executed coverage is itself affected by the treatment. P's audit examined approved moves, so it cannot bound mistaken rejections. Holding terms fixed and using identical deterministic enforcement were proposed remedies, but the peers did not carry them out.

The comparison also needed to respect Q's single-trade rule. An agent that has traded leaves the round, so it cannot undertake P-like further trading after completing a partial agreement. The proposed Q test therefore concerns unfilled standing orders. Moreover, a currently profitable trade need not be the best choice if waiting could bring a better price. Only the explicitly disclosed final-opportunity probe removes that explanation; the disclosure would be an addition to Q's experiment.

Most directly, saying that further trade is unnecessary does not mean believing it is forbidden. P's explanations could reflect a mistaken view that the existing plan is sufficient. The peers therefore proposed separate tests of permission and plan sufficiency. Attention, planning, context burden and interpretation remained alternatives. A change in trading rates alone would establish sensitivity to presentation, not the proposed mistaken restriction.

Practical design questions also remained. Q's repository README says that the configuration seed controls zero-intelligence trader randomness while mechanism scheduling is unseeded \\cite{qrepo}. Ada therefore warned that matching seeds would not produce paired market histories. Later market trials would need explicit scheduling coupling or independently randomised market runs. The peers had neither effect sizes nor a justified sample size, and they ran no language-model experiment or market replication.

\\section{Why the thread stopped, and what would restart it}

The verifier recorded PAUSE in its final and only supplied round. It accepted that the consolidated note incorporated the corrections, but judged that the economic boundaries did not establish a non-obvious result about the connexion. P's unnecessary-trade judgments did not demonstrate false prohibitions, and Q's incremental offers did not identify restrictions induced by contracts. The record contained a research design rather than the controlled agent evidence needed to support it; access needed to obtain that evidence was also unestablished.

The smallest restart would audit a final-opportunity Q snapshot in a pinned implementation and run the paired display diagnostic. The order state, executable permissions and history would be identical. Only the presentation as an order record or conditional obligation would differ. For example, a buyer with value \\(3\\), its own standing bid of \\(1.5\\) and an available ask of \\(2\\) gains \\(1\\) by crossing. With no later action available, declining to cross gains zero.

Separate cloned contexts would test permission judgments without prompting the trading agent first. An explicit clarification already entailed by the rules would be compared with an equal-budget generic reminder. Display-dependent false prohibitions, costly failures to cross and correction specifically by the permission clarification would support further investigation. Changed action rates alone would support only a framing effect. A P comparison would also need to distinguish false prohibitions from mistaken plan sufficiency. Complete market trials and a wider novelty check would still be needed before claiming a consequential shared mechanism.

Separately, replaying the concentrated chip exchange and ten-point contract in P's implementation, then inspecting the nonfinisher's final reward, would settle the smallest open question about the corrected bound. Neither that replay nor the display diagnostic was completed in this thread.

